    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d497">

                <pb facs="0453" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg446"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9528: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-19T12:00:00+08:00">August 19,
                        1937—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received August 20—6:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>467. The informant cited in Embassy’s 460, August 18, noon,<anchor n="44"
                        xml:id="d497-f1"/> states that Chinese Communist troops are already in
                    Suiyuan moving toward the Chahar front for guerilla acting [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">warfare?</hi>], thus confirming similar information
                    reported in our 404, August 12, 9 a.m.</p>
                <p>2. Further word that Chinese Communists are joining with the Government to form a
                    popular front has been received from an official <pb facs="0454" n="447"
                        xml:id="pg447"/> who recently attended a social affair given by Chiang
                    Kai-shek at which were present at one table Feng Yu Hsiang, Yen Hsi Shan, Pai
                    Chung Hsi, Chu Teh, Mao Tze Tung and Chou En Lai.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d497-f2"
                    /></p>
                <p>Repeated to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d497-f1" n="44" xml:id="d497-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; the Soviet Military Attaché in China was cited.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d497-f2" n="45" xml:id="d497-f2-content">
                <p>The last three were Chinese Communist leaders.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
